# Task: C2 (constants) round: owner decisions 18 and 19, plus the vacuity audit fixes

Contract: C2, the closed constant-initializer subset (files: ConcreteSpec/Spec/Const.lean, ConcreteSpec/Proofs/Const.lean, the C2 lines of ConcreteSpec/README.md). This clone is on branch wip/spec-c2-audit, created from the integrated wip/spec-core. This brief overrides the common rule "do not edit Const.lean": you are to edit it.

## Part A: owner decisions of 2026-11-08 (15:08 UTC, the 29 answers, "all yes"). Encode exactly these; they are decided.

**Item 18 (constant initializers; revises TYPE-22).** Initializers may use *fallible* primitive operations, within a *closed grammar*: no calls, no loops, no effects. Success is *kernel-checked*: every initializer is evaluated at compile time (used or not), and a constant whose evaluation fails, or runs out of fuel, is refused.
- Extend the initializer grammar with the fallible scalar operations Spec/Scalar.lean already defines, reusing those definitions (do not define a second arithmetic): the plain operations `plain` / `plainNeg` (add, subtract, multiply, divide, remainder, negation, shifts; failures `Overflow`, `DivisionByZero`, `InvalidShift`, OD114 division) and the fallible conversion `tryConvert`. At compile time a failure of any of them refuses the constant (it never becomes a trap). The `checked` forms return `Result<T, ArithmeticError>`, which is not a type of the current constant universe (`CTy`): do not widen `CTy` (that is an owner decision, see the module doc); list "checked forms in initializers need a Result constant type" under QUESTIONS instead.
- Keep the grammar closed: no calls, loops, effects, state, authority or target dependence (the target profile only where the scalar operations already depend on it, e.g. widths; say which).
- Evaluation becomes partial and fuel-bounded: `eval` returns a value, a failure (which operation, at which sub-expression) or out-of-fuel. Every operation still terminates; fuel bounds the evaluation as the decision says.
- Admission (`ConstsAdmitted`) becomes: the list types (as now) AND every initializer evaluates successfully within the fuel. A failure or fuel exhaustion refuses the constant, whether or not the constant is used anywhere. Make admission decidable so that `decide` (kernel reduction, never `native_decide`) checks a concrete program's constants: that is what "kernel-checked" means here.
- Restate the laws: `eval_total` no longer holds for the widened subset; replace it with (i) totality for the old fallible-free fragment (keep it, it is still true and useful), (ii) admitted ⇒ every constant has a value of its declared type (`evalConsts_sound`), (iii) admission ⇔ typed ∧ evaluates within fuel, with the refusal side naming the failing constant and its failure.
- Examples: Accepted: an initializer using division that succeeds (e.g. `100 / 7`) and an unused constant whose initializer succeeds. Refused: `1 / 0`, a signed overflow (`i8` `127 + 1`), an out-of-range shift, and an UNUSED constant whose initializer fails (refused although never used: this is the TYPE-22 revision). One example where fuel runs out (a fuel bound too small for a deep but finite initializer) is refused.
- Update the module doc comment: remove "a question for the owner, not decided here" about failing operations; cite "owner decision 2026-11-08, item 18 (revises TYPE-22)".

**Item 19 (constant order).** A constant's initializer may name a constant written later in the source; cycles are refused, and the refusal shows the chain. Core still receives constants in dependency order from elaboration, but now specify what elaboration must do:
- define the dependency relation of a module's constants (from the source declaration order, which may name later constants), the sorting obligation (elaboration supplies a dependency order, i.e. a permutation of the source list in which each initializer refers only to earlier constants; README obligation O1), and a refusal for a cycle that carries the cycle as a list of constant identities `c₀ → c₁ → … → c₀`, each edge an actual reference;
- laws: if the source has no cycle, a dependency order exists and admission over any such order gives the same values (order-independence of the result); if it has a cycle, the refusal's chain is a real cycle of the reference relation;
- examples: Accepted forward reference (`A = B + 1` written before `B = 2`); Refused two-constant cycle and self-reference, each with its chain; planted counter-example: a "chain" that is not a cycle of the relation fails the chain law.
- Update the doc comment: remove "is a question for the owner, not decided here" about order; cite "owner decision 2026-11-08, item 19".

Update the C2 lines in ConcreteSpec/README.md (the "two owner questions" sentence is now decided by items 18 and 19).

## Part B: vacuity audit fixes

The audit (Codex, Kimi, agy; read-only) is in _review/AUDIT_FINDINGS.md: fix every VACUOUS or WEAK verdict on C2's files (`admitted_copy`, `ofC_copy`, `ConstsAdmitted` and the non-Copy Refused example, `checkedNeg_inRange`, and any other C2 row). For each, either (a) strengthen it into a real law a mutated rule would falsify, or (b) demote it to a named helper (`private`, or a name ending in `_inv` / `_proj`, with a doc comment saying so) when a real law covering the property exists or is added. Note the conflict between agy's suggested fix for `ConstsAdmitted` (define it by typing only) and item 18 (admission now requires successful evaluation): item 18 governs; make the typing part and the evaluation part separate, named and each non-vacuous. For the non-Copy Refused example, make it refused for the reason it claims (types agree, only Copy fails). Where reviewers disagree, decide from the statement and say why.

If any part needs a rule no decided row answers, stop that part and list it under QUESTIONS with options and a recommendation.

## Vacuity rule (owner, binding)
No law may hold because a guard or a definition makes it trivially true (for example a run-time re-check of the invariant inside the operation, a theorem that restates its definition, or a hypothesis that can never hold). For every law you add or change, give (a) a non-trivial instance: an example where its hypotheses hold, and (b) a planted counter-example: a mutated rule or a bad state that fails it, written as an `example` with a negation. Say in your final message, per law, which examples witness (a) and (b).

## Best long-term design (owner rule, binding)
Judge and fix everything by the best long-term design. Never cut corners for speed, a smaller diff or fewer rounds. Implement the structural fix, not a containment patch: no law that holds by construction or restates its definition, no weakened statement, no premise that switches off an obligation. If staging is truly unavoidable, say so in your final message and record the long-term design and the roadmap row it belongs to (text for the coordinator; do not edit ROADMAP.md).

## Evidence tags (for the coming vacuity gate)
Tag every instance and planted counter-example in its doc comment: `/-- Instance (law `Fully.Qualified.name`). ... -/` and `/-- Planted (law `Fully.Qualified.name`). ... -/` (always the fully-qualified Lean name, never a short name; one example may list several laws). Mark helpers `/-- Helper (inversion|projection|congruence|arithmetic). ... -/` (and make them `private` or suffix `_inv` / `_proj`), representation lemmas `/-- Representation lemma. ... -/`, and named obligations on later components `/-- Obligation (discharged at slice N, R-xxxx). ... -/`. Keep the existing `Accepted (rule ...)` / `Refused (rule ...)` tags.

